Features: Describing the Data 
Before a model can learn, every training example must be described in a form that the computer 
can process. These descriptions are known as features. 
A feature is any measurable characteristic that may help distinguish one example from another. In 
the spam-filter example, possible features might include: 
 the number of hyperlinks contained in the message;  
 whether the sender appears in the recipient's contact list;  
 the number of capital letters;  
 the frequency of words such as free, winner, or urgent;  
 the presence of suspicious attachments;  
 the length of the message.  
Each email can therefore be represented by a collection of numerical or categorical values

 Storage — used for training data, model files, logs, and other information;  
 Networking — allows computing devices and other systems to communicate;  
 Software infrastructure — operating systems, drivers, libraries, distributed-computing 
frameworks, and model-serving software;  
 Data-center infrastructure — power, cooling, physical facilities, and other resources 
required to operate the equipment.  
These components are closely related. For example, adding more computing devices does not 
automatically increase performance proportionally. The devices must be able to exchange 
information efficiently, and sufficient memory, storage, and network capacity must be available. 
The infrastructure supporting an LLM is therefore a system of interconnected resources, rather

