These components do not necessarily replace one another. A modern LLM system may use all 
three, with different components performing different functions. 
CPUs 
A CPU is a general-purpose processor capable of executing a wide variety of instructions. CPUs 
are essential components of servers and other computers used in LLM infrastructure. They can 
perform tasks such as: 
 managing operating-system functions;  
 controlling application processes;  
 handling input and output;  
 managing data;  
 coordinating other computing resources;  
 and performing calculations that are not particularly well suited to highly parallel 
processing.  
A CPU can also execute an LLM. However, for many modern models, using CPUs alone may not

practical consequences. They affect:  
 response time;  
 number of users that can be served;  
 system scalability;  
 infrastructure cost;  
 energy consumption;  
 and the feasibility of deploying particular models.  
For example, an organization may have a choice between deploying a relatively small model 
using modest infrastructure, or deploying a much larger model requiring substantially greater 
computing resources. The technically most powerful model is not necessarily the most 
appropriate solution for an organization. 
The infrastructure decision must therefore be considered together with the application's 
requirements, expected workload, cost constraints, security requirements, and desired level of 
performance. 
