Model Size and Infrastructure Requirements 
In general, larger models tend to require more computing and memory resources than smaller 
models. However, model size is not the only factor determining infrastructure requirements. 
Other factors include:  
 the number of simultaneous users;  
 the length of prompts and responses;  
 the amount of context supplied to the model;  
 the frequency of requests;  
 the required response time;  
 and the software and hardware used to execute the model.  
For example, a relatively small model serving a very large number of users may require 
substantial infrastructure, while a larger model used occasionally by a small internal group may

Operating an LLM internally can provide greater control over infrastructure; model deployment; 
data location; access; and operational policies. However, greater control also means greater 
responsibility. The organization must provide the expertise and resources needed to operate the 
environment effectively. 
Security and Privacy 
Security and privacy can significantly influence infrastructure decisions. An organization may 
need to determine: 
 where data is processed;  
 where it is stored;  
 who can access it;  
 how information is transmitted;  
 and what controls are required to protect it.  
