Combining Several Integration Patterns 
Information systems need not follow only one of these patterns. A single enterprise application 
could allow a user to: 
1. ask a natural-language question;  
2. retrieve information from an internal database;  
3. retrieve relevant information from external sources;  
4. ask the LLM to compare the two sources;  
5. and, if appropriate and authorized, invoke an existing application function.  
A single enterprise application may combine several of these patterns; for example, retrieving

 answer questions using the information supplied by the application;  
 distinguish between known information and assumptions;  
 avoid revealing confidential customer information;  
 use a specified response format;  
 and refer the user to a human representative when the request falls outside the system's 
authorized capabilities.  
These instructions are different from the user's individual request. 
The user may ask: “Give me the customer's complete history.” The system may have rules that 
restrict which information the employee is authorized to receive. The LLM should therefore not 
be relied upon as the sole mechanism for enforcing access control. The application and its 
underlying information systems should determine which information may be supplied to the

often dynamic. The system may construct a different model input for every user request, 
depending on: 
 who the user is;  
 what the user has requested;  
 what information is relevant;  
 what information the user is authorized to access;  
 what has already been discussed;  
 and what external information is available.  
Two users may therefore enter the same natural-language question but cause the system to 
construct different inputs for the LLM. 
For example, two employees may ask: “What is the status of this customer account?” One 
employee may be authorized to see financial information while another may not. The application 
should retrieve and supply only the information appropriate to each user's authorization.

