The difference is therefore not necessarily in the LLM itself. It can arise from the information and 
instructions supplied to the model by the surrounding information system. 
Prompt Construction and Application Logic 
Prompt construction is not simply a matter of writing a clever prompt. Within an information 
system, it is part of the application's processing logic. The application may need to: 
1. receive the user's request;  
2. identify the user and relevant permissions;  
3. determine what information is required;  
4. retrieve appropriate information;  
5. construct the model's context;  
6. send the resulting input to the LLM;  
7. receive the generated response;

be quite different. Nevertheless, both types of indexes serve the same broad systems objective: 
avoid examining all stored information when a search is performed. 
Modern vector-search systems use specialized indexing and similarity-search techniques to make 
retrieval efficient even when the collection contains very large numbers of vectors.  
Who Performs the Embedding and Indexing? 
As with chunking, there is a distinction between design decisions and execution. The developers 
or system architects decide: 
 which embedding model to use;  
 which information should be embedded;  
 which vector-storage or search technology to use;  
 what metadata should accompany each chunk;  
 and how the index should be updated.

