Existing Application Services as an Alternative to Direct SQL 
Generating SQL is not the only way an LLM can access structured organizational information.

 It may use predefined application services instead of permitting arbitrary SQL. 
 It may require human confirmation before sensitive operations are executed. 
These mechanisms illustrate a general principle: The LLM should operate within the security and 
business controls of the information system rather than bypassing them. 
LLMs and Structured Data Beyond Relational Databases 
Although SQL provides a particularly clear example, the same principle applies to other 
structured information sources. An organization may use: 
 NoSQL databases;  
 data warehouses;  
 data lakes;  
 business-intelligence systems;  
 enterprise APIs;  
 ERP systems;  
 CRM systems;  
 or other structured information services.

 what schema and business metadata should be provided;  
 which users are authorized to access which information;  
 which operations should be read-only;  
 which operations require confirmation;  
 how generated queries should be validated;  
 and how the resulting answers should be evaluated.  
These decisions are familiar Information Systems concerns. The introduction of an LLM does not 
eliminate them. Instead, it creates a new natural-language layer through which users can interact 
with the existing systems. 
This is one of the central themes of this chapter: LLM integration extends the ways in which 
users can interact with information systems, but the underlying systems remain responsible 
for storing, processing, securing, and governing organizational information.

The LLM could then ask additional questions about: 
 the sources of complaints;  
 the information recorded;  
 the people responsible for processing them;  
 the rules for assigning complaints;  
 the required response times;  
 and the reports required by management.  
In this way, an LLM can support a more structured conversation than a user might have 
independently.  
It can also help the analyst organize the information obtained from such conversations. For 
example, a collection of interview notes might be transformed into preliminary categories such 
as: 
Actors: Customer, Customer-Service Employee, Supervisor 
Business Processes: Complaint Submission, Complaint Classification, Complaint Assignment, 
Complaint Resolution

Therefore: An LLM can assist with requirements elicitation, but it does not automatically replace 
the human requirements-elicitation process. 
The Analyst's Role 
The role of the systems analyst may consequently change rather than disappear. Instead of 
spending as much time performing routine activities such as transcribing interviews, organizing 
notes, or preparing preliminary requirement lists, the analyst may spend more time: 
 determining which stakeholders should be consulted;  
 designing the elicitation process;  
 evaluating information obtained from different sources;  
 resolving conflicts between stakeholders;  
 identifying unstated requirements;  
 validating the LLM's interpretations;

