 what the major classes represent;  
 which relationships exist;  
 where inheritance is used;  
 which parts of the model may require clarification;  
 or whether particular requirements appear to be represented.  
This can be useful when a system contains many models or when different stakeholders need 
explanations at different levels of technical detail. 
Identifying Inconsistencies Between Requirements and Models 
Another potentially useful application is comparing different representations of the same system. 
Suppose the requirements document states that: A customer may have many orders, but a data 
or class model appears to represent a one-to-one relationship between Customer and Order. An 
LLM can help identify this apparent inconsistency.

