A system designer must determine how these requirements should be implemented. Possible 
design decisions include: 
 what major software components are required;  
 how employees and managers will interact with the system;  
 where employee and vacation-request data will be stored;  
 how the system will communicate with the organization's existing HR system;  
 how authentication and authorization will be handled;  
 and how the system will be integrated with other organizational systems.  
An LLM can assist by taking the requirements and suggesting possible design alternatives. For 
example, it might propose an architecture containing: 
User Interface → Application Services → Database

 what information is exchanged;  
 how requests and responses are structured;  
 how errors are handled;  
 how users or applications are authenticated;  
 and what happens when one component is unavailable.  
An LLM can help generate preliminary API specifications, example requests and responses, 
interface documentation, and explanations of how components interact. It can also examine an 
existing API specification and explain how it can be used by another component. 
Nevertheless, interface design involves precise technical and organizational decisions. Generated 
specifications must therefore be reviewed against the actual requirements and implementation 
constraints. 
Designing the Database

316 
However, the database designer must still consider issues such as data integrity, transaction 
requirements, performance, indexing, security, and the organization's existing database 
environment. 
The same principle that applied to UML modeling therefore applies to database design: An LLM 
can generate a plausible design, but plausibility is not the same as correctness. 
Designing User Interfaces 
LLMs can also contribute to user-interface design. A designer can describe the intended users and 
tasks and ask the LLM to propose:  
 screen layouts;  
 navigation structures;  
 forms;  
 terminology;  
 validation messages;  
 or alternative interaction approaches.  
The LLM may also generate preliminary interface code based on a description. For example:

It can also help identify inconsistencies between design documents and other project artifacts. 
The Designer's Role 
The increasing ability of LLMs to generate design alternatives may change the role of system and 
software designers. A designer may spend less time producing routine documentation or 
manually creating initial versions of designs. At the same time, greater emphasis may be placed 
on: 
 understanding organizational requirements;  
 evaluating architectural alternatives;  
 identifying risks;  
 validating assumptions;  
 considering long-term consequences;  
 and making decisions that involve trade-offs between competing objectives.  
