Code Completion and Transformation 
LLMs are not limited to generating complete programs from scratch. They can also assist while a 
programmer is writing code. For example, a programmer may begin writing a function and ask

programmer is writing code. For example, a programmer may begin writing a function and ask 
the LLM to complete it. The model can infer the likely intention from the surrounding code and 
suggest the remaining statements. 
An LLM can also transform existing code. Examples include: 
 converting code from one programming language to another;  
 translating between programming frameworks;  
 modernizing older programming constructs;  
 refactoring repetitive code;  
 adding comments;  
 or restructuring a program while attempting to preserve its behavior.  
These capabilities can be particularly valuable when organizations maintain legacy systems. A 
software organization may have applications written many years ago using technologies that are

way quantities or prices are handled. 
It can also suggest test cases designed to reproduce or isolate the problem. This can reduce the 
time required to investigate routine programming errors. However, the LLM's diagnosis is a 
hypothesis, not proof that the suggested cause is correct. The programmer must reproduce the 
problem, examine the actual execution, and test the proposed solution. 
Generating Tests Along with Code 
An LLM can also generate test cases for code that it has produced or that a programmer has 
written. For example, after generating a function that calculates an order total, the LLM might 
suggest tests involving: 
 an empty order;  
 one product;  
 several products;  
