The Programmer's Role 
The ability to generate code raises an obvious question: If an LLM can write programs, what is 
the role of the programmer? The answer is not simply that programmers disappear. 
Programming involves more than writing syntactically correct statements. A programmer must 
understand: 
 what the system is supposed to do;  
 the assumptions behind the requirements;  
 the architecture in which the code will operate;  
 security and privacy requirements;  
 performance constraints;  
 interactions with other components;  
 and the consequences of errors.  
An LLM may generate a function that appears to satisfy a particular request while violating an 
important requirement that was not explicitly stated in the prompt. The programmer therefore

The quality of generated code depends heavily on the information available to the LLM. A simple 
request such as: "Write a function to calculate an order total" may be insufficient for a real 
application. The LLM may need information about: 
 the programming language;  
 the database schema;  
 existing classes or functions;  
 business rules;  
 currency and taxation rules;  
 error-handling conventions;  
 security requirements;  
 and the interfaces of other components.  
This is consistent with the discussion of prompt construction in Chapter 11. In a professional 
development environment, the LLM may receive not only the programmer's immediate request 
but also relevant project context.

but also relevant project context. 
Thus, effective code generation is not simply a matter of writing a clever prompt. It also depends 
on providing the model with appropriate and accurate development context. 
Security and Quality Considerations 
Generated code must be examined for security vulnerabilities and other quality problems. An 
LLM may generate code that: 
 does not properly validate input;  
 exposes sensitive information;  
 uses insecure programming practices;  
 handles authentication incorrectly;  
 or fails under unusual conditions.  
The fact that the code compiles or passes a small number of tests does not establish that it is 
secure. Organizations should therefore apply their normal software-engineering practices to

software and its tests may be produced using the same underlying model. Consequently, 
organizations should not assume that automatically generated tests provide independent evidence 
that the generated software is correct. 
Generating Test Cases 
One straightforward application of an LLM is the generation of candidate test cases from 
requirements. 
Suppose a requirement for a university registration system states: "A student cannot register for 
a course if the student has not completed all required prerequisites." 
An LLM can generate several possible test cases, including: 
 a student who has completed all prerequisites;  
 a student who has completed some but not all prerequisites;  
 a student who has completed none of the prerequisites;  
 a course with no prerequisites;

 a likely programming error;  
 an incorrect input;  
 a missing dependency;  
 an unexpected data condition;  
 or a possible configuration problem.  
It can also suggest additional tests that might help distinguish between competing explanations. 
As with debugging discussed in Section 13.4, these suggestions are hypotheses that require 
verification. 
The LLM does not observe the complete execution environment simply because it has been given 
an error message. The actual cause must be established through testing and investigation. 
Regression Testing and Software Maintenance 
LLMs can also support regression testing. When a program is modified, previously functioning 
behavior may accidentally be affected. Regression testing attempts to detect such unintended

LLMs and Quality Assurance 
Software quality involves more than testing whether individual functions produce expected 
outputs. Quality assurance may also involve: 
 security;  
 performance;  
 reliability;  
 usability;  
 maintainability;  
 accessibility;  
 compatibility;  
 and compliance with organizational standards.  
LLMs can assist with some of these activities. For example, an LLM can review source code and 
identify possible security weaknesses or maintainability problems. It can suggest performance-
related issues or generate documentation that supports maintenance. 
However, these suggestions should not be treated as substitutes for specialized testing and 
analysis. A security-sensitive application may require dedicated security testing. A high-

