 determining what should be tested;  
 identifying important risks;  
 evaluating whether the tests reflect actual business requirements;  
 interpreting unexpected results;  
 designing tests for unusual situations;  
 and determining whether the evidence is sufficient to conclude that the system is ready 
for use.  
The tester therefore becomes less focused on manually producing every test artifact and more 
focused on test strategy, critical evaluation, and quality assurance. 
Testing AI-Based Features 
There is an additional challenge when the system being tested contains an LLM itself. Traditional 
software testing often assumes that the same input should produce the same output according to 
precisely defined rules.

Software development produces many forms of documentation throughout the development life 
cycle, including: 
 user requirements documents;  
 functional and non-functional requirements;  
 systems analysis and design descriptions;  
 models and diagrams, such as UML diagrams;  
 system architecture descriptions;  
 program documentation;  
 API documentation;  
 database documentation;  
 installation and deployment instructions;  
 user manuals;  
 and maintenance documentation.  
LLMs can assist with generating, organizing, explaining, and updating many of these documents 
from requirements, models, source code, database schemas, and other development artifacts. 
