and produce higher-level descriptions. For example, an analyst might provide the source code of

system may contain business rules that are not explicitly documented in the code, and some 
outdated design decisions may need to be reconsidered rather than reproduced. 
Database and Data Migration 
The same principles apply to databases. An LLM can assist in understanding an existing database 
schema and preparing migration materials. For example, it may help: 
 document existing tables and relationships;  
 identify dependencies;  
 generate SQL migration scripts;  
 translate SQL between database systems;  
 suggest mappings between old and new schemas;  
 and explain potential data-conversion problems.  
Suppose an organization is migrating from one DBMS to another. An LLM might help translate 
SQL statements and identify constructs that are specific to the original system.

331 
 which programs use the attribute;  
 which reports depend on it;  
 which APIs expose it;  
 which database views reference it;  
 and which other systems receive the associated data.  
An LLM can assist by analyzing available source code and documentation and identifying 
possible dependencies. This can help developers formulate an initial impact analysis. 
However, the completeness of the result depends on the information available to the LLM. If an 
external system or undocumented process is not represented in the available material, the LLM 
cannot reliably identify its dependency. 
Documentation as Part of the Development Process 
The examples above suggest that documentation should not be regarded as something produced

 generating acceptance criteria;  
 preparing sprint documentation;  
 generating preliminary code;  
 creating test cases;  
 summarizing meetings;  
 and documenting completed work.  
For example, an LLM can help transform a user story into possible acceptance criteria. The 
development team can then review and modify these criteria before they become part of the 
sprint. The important point is that the LLM can accelerate activities within an Agile process 
without changing the fundamental principles of Agile development. 
Faster production of artifacts does not necessarily mean that the project itself should move 
proportionally faster. If requirements, code, and tests can all be generated quickly, the bottleneck

The Changing Role of IS Professionals 
The preceding sections have shown that LLMs can perform an increasing amount of routine 
analytical and generative work. This may change the distribution of activities among IS 
professionals. 
Systems Analysts: 
Analysts may spend less time on routine activities such as transcribing interviews, organizing 
notes, and preparing preliminary requirements. More emphasis may instead be placed on: 
 identifying the appropriate stakeholders;  
 understanding the underlying business problem;  
 resolving conflicts between stakeholders;  
 identifying unstated requirements;  
 evaluating LLM-generated interpretations;  
 and validating requirements.  
The analyst therefore increasingly becomes a director and evaluator of the analysis process,

The analyst therefore increasingly becomes a director and evaluator of the analysis process, 
rather than simply a producer of analysis artifacts. 
Software Developers: 
Programmers may spend less time writing routine code manually and more time: 
 defining development tasks;  
 providing appropriate context;  
 reviewing generated code;  
 integrating components;  
 testing;  
 debugging;  
 and making technical decisions.  
The ability to understand programming remains important because a professional who 
understands software can evaluate generated code much more effectively than someone who 
simply accepts it. 

