This distinction is consistent with the organizational responsibilities discussed in Chapter 12. The 
purpose here is different: the emphasis is on accountability for the consequences and 
appropriateness of LLM use. 
Balancing Benefits and Risks 
Ethical evaluation does not necessarily mean avoiding every application that involves some risk. 
LLMs can provide substantial benefits, including: 
 improving access to information;  
 assisting employees;  
 supporting people with disabilities;  
 improving communication;  
 automating repetitive activities;  
 and helping organizations provide services more efficiently.  
The relevant question is whether the expected benefits justify the risks and whether those risks

alone. An organization that uses LLMs needs mechanisms for deciding how these technologies 
may be used, under what conditions, and with what controls. 
These mechanisms are part of LLM governance. Governance does not mean that every use of an 
LLM must be centrally controlled. Rather, it provides an organizational framework within which 
different users and business units can use LLM capabilities while remaining consistent with 
organizational requirements. 
Why Governance Is Needed 
An organization may use LLMs in many different ways. For example: 
 employees may use an LLM to draft documents;  
 customer-service applications may generate responses;  
 analysts may use LLMs to summarize information;  
 developers may use LLMs to generate code;

 new information sources may be connected;  
 retrieval mechanisms may be modified;  
 or the system may be given additional capabilities.  
A change that appears technically small can sometimes have significant effects on system 
behavior. Organizations should therefore determine which changes require formal evaluation or 
approval. This is particularly important for high-risk applications. 
Governance of External Services 
When an organization uses an external LLM service, governance must also address the 
relationship with the service provider. The organization may need to understand: 
 what data is sent to the service;  
 how the service handles that data;  
 what level of service is provided;  
 how changes to the service are communicated;

 what level of service is provided;  
 how changes to the service are communicated;  
 and what responsibilities remain with the organization.  
The organization cannot simply assume that using an external service transfers all responsibility 
to the provider. The organization remains responsible for determining whether the service is 
appropriate for its particular Information System and intended use. 
Employee Training and Awareness 
Governance also depends on the people who use LLMs. Employees should understand the 
organization's policies and the limitations of LLM-based systems. 
Training may address: 
 appropriate and inappropriate uses;  
 protection of confidential information;  
 verification of generated information;  
