Identifying the Use Case 
The first step is to clearly define what the LLM-based system is intended to do.  
For example, consider an organization planning to introduce an LLM-based customer-service 
assistant. The organization should determine: 
 who will use the system;  
 what questions it will answer;  
 what information it will access;  
 whether it will only provide information or also perform actions;  
 and what decisions, if any, may be influenced by its outputs.  
A clearly defined use case provides the basis for identifying the relevant risks. 
Identifying Assets and Stakeholders 
The organization should next identify what may be affected by the system. These may include: 
 personal information;  
 confidential organizational information;  
 databases and documents;

 personal information;  
 confidential organizational information;  
 databases and documents;  
 application services;  
 employees;  
 customers;  
 business partners;  
 and the organization's reputation.  
Different stakeholders may experience different consequences if the system fails or is misused. 
For example, an incorrect response from a customer-service assistant may inconvenience a 
customer, while disclosure of confidential customer information could create a much more 
serious problem. 

